   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Three writing systems",
     "texto": [
      "Japanese mixes three systems. **hiragana** (ひらがな) is used for word endings, particles and any word. **katakana** (カタカナ) is used for foreign words. **kanji** (漢字) are characters of Chinese origin.",
      "With hiragana you can already write **any** Japanese word, which is why it is learned first. It has 46 basic signs."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "How it sounds",
     "texto": [
      "Each sign is a **syllable**, not a letter: か is «ka», not «k».",
      "The five vowels always **sound the same**: a, i, u, e, o. This is a big advantage: they are simpler than English vowels.",
      "The rows always follow the same order: a, i, u, e, o. There are only three surprises in this lesson: し is read **shi** (not «see»), ち is read **chi** (not «tee») and つ is read **tsu** (not «too»).",
      "In the pronunciation you will see, the **h** is breathed out very softly, like a light English «h», the **sh** sounds like the English «sh» and the **z** vibrates, like a buzzing sound."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
